.3 2,103 137,775 14,265 9.3 81,659\nL86@P0: 2010. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 237,830 153,889 64.7 139,064 58.5 2,206 136,858 14,825 9.6 83,941\nL87@P0: 2011. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 239,618 153,617 64.1 139,869 58.4 2,254 137,615 13,747 8.9 86,001\nL88@P0-1: 1HOUSEHOLD DATA\nL89@P1: ANNUAL AVERAGES\nL90@P1: 1. Employment status of the civilian noninstitutional population, 1955 to date — Continued\nL91@P1: [Numbers in thousands]\nL92@P1: Year\nL93@P1: Civilian\nL94@P1: noninstitu\u0002tional\nL95@P1: population\nL96@P1: Civilian labor force\nL97@P1: Not\nL98@P1: in\nL99@P1: labor\nL100@P1: force\nL101@P1: Total\nL102@P1: Percent\nL103@P1: of\nL104@P1: population\nL105@P1: Employed Unemployed\nL106@P1: Total\nL107@P1: Percent\nL108@P1: of\nL109@P1: population\nL110@P1: Agri\u0002culture\nL111@P1: Nonagri\u0002cultural\nL112@P1: industries\nL113@P1: Number\nL114@P1: Percent\nL115@P1: of\nL116@P1: labor\nL117@P1: force\nL118@P1: 2012. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 243,284 154,975 63.7 142,469 58.6 2,186 140,283 12,506 8.1 88,310\nL119@P1: 2013. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 245,679 155,389 63.2 143,929 58.6 2,130 141,799 11,460 7.4 90,290\nL120@P1: 2014. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 247,947 155,922 62.9 146,305 59.0 2,237 144,068 9,617 6.2 92,025\nL121@P1: 2015. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 250,801 157,130 62.7 148,834 59.3 2,422 146,411 8,296 5.3 93,671\nL122@P1: 2016. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 253,538 159,187 62.8 151,436 59.7 2,460 148,976 7,751 4.9 94,351\nL123@P1: 2017. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 255,079 160,320 62.9 153,337 60.1 2,454 150,883 6,982 4.4 94,759\nL124@P1: 2018. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 257,791 162,075 62.9 155,761 60.4 2,425 153,336 6,314 3.9 95,716\nL125@P1: 2019. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 259,175 163,539 63.1 157,538 60.8 2,425 155,113 6,001 3.7 95,636\nL126@P1: 2020. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 260,329 160,742 61.7 147,795 56.8 2,349 145,446 12,947 8.1 99,587\nL127@P1: 2021. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 261,445 161,204 61.7 152,581 58.4 2,291 150,290 8,623 5.3 100,241\nL128@P1: 2022. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 263,973 164,287 62.2 158,291 60.0 2,290 156,001 5,996 3.6 99,686\nL129@P1: 2023. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 266,942 167,116 62.6 161,037 60.3 2,264 158,772 6,080 3.6 99,826\nL130@P1: 2024. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 268,571 168,106 62.6 161,346 60.1 2,254 159,091 6,761 4.0 100,465\nL131@P1: 2025. . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . . 273,653 170,807 62.4 163,493 59.7 2,225 161,268 7,314 4.3 102,846\nL132@P1: NOTE: Revisions to population controls and other changes can affect the comparability of labor force levels over time. In recent years, for example, updated population\nL133@P1: controls have been introduced annually with the release of January data. Information about historical comparability is online at\nL134@P1: https://www.bls.gov/cps/documentation.htm#comp. Annual estimates for 2025 are 11-month averages that exclude October. (Data for October 2025 were not collected due\nL135@P1: to the federal government shutdown.) As a result, 2025 annual estimates are not strictly comparable with annual averages for other years.\nL136@P1: 2\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n21-476 303 Creative LLC v. Elenis (06/30/2023) (https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/21-476_c185.pdf)\nciteturn66view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 70; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/22pdf/21-476_c185.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 2242\nL0@P0: \nL1@P0: \nL2@P0: \nL3@P0: \nL4@P0: \nL5@P0: \nL6@P0: (Slip Opinion) OCTOBER TERM, 2022 1\nL7@P0: Syllabus\nL8@P0: NOTE: Where it is feasible, a syllabus (headnote) will be released, as is\nL9@P0: being done in connection with this case, at the time the opinion is issued.\nL10@P0: The syllabus constitutes no part of the opinion of the Court but has been\nL11@P0: prepared by the Reporter of Decisions for the convenience of the reader.\nL12@P0: See United States v. Detroit Timber & Lumber Co., 200 U. S. 321, 337.\nL13@P0: SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES\nL14@P0: Syllabus\nL15@P0: 303 CREATIVE LLC ET AL. v. ELENIS ET AL.\nL16@P0: CERTIORARI TO THE UNITED STATES COURT OF APPEALS FOR\nL17@P0: THE TENTH CIRCUIT\nL18@P0: No. 21–476. Argued December 5, 2022—Decided June 30, 2023\nL19@P0: Lorie Smith wants to expand her graphic design business, 303 Creative\nL20@P0: LLC, to include services for couples seeking wedding websites. But\nL21@P0: Ms. Smith worries that Colorado will use the Colorado Anti-Discrimi\u0002nation Act to compel her—in violation of the First Amendment—to cre\u0002ate websites celebrating marriages she does not endorse. To clarify\nL22@P0: her rights, Ms. Smith filed a lawsuit seeking an injunction to prevent\nL23@P0: the State from forcing her to create websites celebrating marriages\nL24@P0: that defy her belief that marriage should be reserved to unions be\u0002tween one man and one woman.\nL25@P0: CADA prohibits all “public accommodations” from denying “the full\nL26@P0: and equal enjoyment” of its goods and services to any customer based\nL27@P0: on his race, creed, disability, sexual orientation, or other statutorily\nL28@P0: enumerated trait. Colo. Rev. Stat. §24–34–601(2)(a). The law defines\nL29@P0: “public accommodation” broadly to include almost every public-facing\nL30@P0: business in the State. §24–34–601(1). Either state officials or private\nL31@P0: citizens may bring actions to enforce the law. §§24–34–306, 24–34–\nL32@P0: 602(1). And a variety of penalties can follow any violation.\nL33@P0: Before the district court, Ms. Smith and the State stipulated to a\nL34@P0: number of facts: Ms. Smith is “willing to work with all people regard\u0002less of classifications such as race, creed, sexual orientation, and gen\u0002der” and “will gladly create custom graphics and websites” for clients\nL35@P0: of any sexual orientation; she will not produce content that “contra\u0002dicts biblical truth” regardless of who orders it; Ms. Smith’s belief that\nL36@P0: marriage is a union between one man and one woman is a sincerely\nL37@P0: held conviction; Ms. Smith provides design services that are “expres\u0002sive” and her “original, customized” creations “contribut[e] to the over\u0002all message” her business conveys “through the websites” it creates;\nL38@P0-1: the wedding websites she plans to create “will be expressive in nature,”\nL39@P1: \nL40@P1: \nL41@P1: \nL42@P1: \nL43@P1: \nL44@P1: 2 303 CREATIVE LLC v. ELENIS\nL45@P1: Syllabus\nL46@P1: will be “customized and tailored” through close collaboration with in\u0002dividual couples, and will “express Ms. Smith’s and 303 Creative’s\nL47@P1: message celebrating and promoting” her view of marriage; viewers of\nL48@P1: Ms. Smith’s websites “will know that the websites are her original art\u0002work;” and “[t]here are numerous companies in the State of Colorado\nL49@P1: and across the nation that offer custom website design services.”\nL50@P1: Ultimately, the district court held that Ms. Smith was not entitled\nL51@P1: to the injunction she sought, and the Tenth Circuit affirmed.\nL52@P1: Held: The First Amendment prohibits Colorado from forcing a website\nL53@P1: designer to create expressive designs speaking messages with which\nL54@P1: the designer disagrees. Pp. 6–26.\nL55@P1: (a) The framers designed the Free Speech Clause of the First\nL56@P1: Amendment to protect the “freedom to think as you will and to speak\nL57@P1: as you think.” Boy Scouts of America v. Dale, 530 U. S. 640, 660–661\nL58@P1: (internal quotation marks omitted). The freedom to speak is among\nL59@P1: our inalienable rights. The freedom of thought and speech is “indis\u0002pensable to the discovery and spread of political truth.” Whitney v.\nL60@P1: California, 274 U. S. 357, 375 (Brandeis, J., concurring). For these\nL61@P1: reasons, “[i]f there is any fixed star in our constitutional constellation,”\nL62@P1: West Virginia Bd. of Ed. v. Barnette, 319 U. S. 624, 642, it is the prin\u0002ciple that the government may not interfere with “an uninhibited mar\u0002ketplace of ideas,” McCullen v. Coakley, 573 U. S. 464, 476 (internal\nL63@P1: quotation marks omitted).\nL64@P1: This Court has previously faced cases where governments have\nL65@P1: sought to test these foundational principles. In Barnette, the Court\nL66@P1: held that the State of West Virginia’s efforts to compel schoolchildren\nL67@P1: to salute the Nation’s flag and recite the Pledge of Allegiance “in\u0002vad[ed] the sphere of intellect and spirit which it is the purpose of the\nL68@P1: First Amendment . . . to reserve from all official control.” 319 U. S., at\nL69@P1: 642. State authorities had “transcend[ed] constitutional limitations on\nL70@P1: their powers.” 319 U. S., at 642. In Hurley v. Irish-American Gay,\nL71@P1: Lesbian and Bisexual Group of Boston, Inc., 515 U. S. 557, the Court\nL72@P1: held that Massachusetts’s public accommodations statute could not be\nL73@P1: used to force veterans organizing a parade in Boston to include a group\nL74@P1: of gay, lesbian, and bisexual individuals because the parade was pro\u0002tected speech, and requiring the veterans to include voices they wished\nL75@P1: to exclude would impermissibly require them to “alter the expressive\nL76@P1: content of their parade.” Id., at 572–573. And in Boy Scouts of America\nL77@P1: v. Dale, when the Boy Scouts sought to exclude assistant scoutmaster\nL78@P1: James Dale from membership after learning he was gay, the Court\nL79@P1: held the Boy Scouts to be “an expressive association” entitled to First\nL80@P1: Amendment protection. 530 U. S., at 656. The Court found that forc\u0002ing the Scouts to include Mr. Dale would undoubtedly “interfere with\nL81@P1-2: [its] choice not to propound a point of view contrary to its beliefs.” Id.,\nL82@P2: \nL83@P2: \nL84@P2: \nL85@P2: Cite as: 600 U. S. ____ (2023) 3\nL86@P2: Syllabus\nL87@P2: at 654.\nL88@P2: These cases illustrate that the First Amendment protects an indi\u0002vidual’s right to speak his mind regardless of whether the government\nL89@P2: considers his speech sensible and well intentioned or deeply “mis\u0002guided,” Hurley, 515 U. S., at 574, and likely to cause “anguish” or “in\u0002calculable grief,” Snyder v. Phelps, 562 U. S. 443, 456. Generally, too,\nL90@P2: the government may not compel a person to speak its own preferred\nL91@P2: messages. See Tinker v. Des Moines Independent Community School\nL92@P2: Dist., 393 U. S. 503, 505. Pp. 6–9.\nL93@P2: (b) Applying these principles to the parties’ stipulated facts, the\nL94@P2: Court agrees with the Tenth Circuit that the wedding websites Ms.\nL95@P2: Smith seeks to create qualify as pure speech protected by the First\nL96@P2: Amendment under this Court’s precedents. Ms. Smith’s websites will\nL97@P2: express and communicate ideas—namely, those that “celebrate and\nL98@P2: promote the couple’s wedding and unique love story” and those that\nL99@P2: “celebrat[e] and promot[e]” what Ms. Smith understands to be a mar\u0002riage. Speech conveyed over the internet, like all other manner of\nL100@P2: speech, qualifies for the First Amendment’s protections. And the\nL101@P2: Court agrees with the Tenth Circuit that the wedding websites Ms.\nL102@P2: Smith seeks to create involve her speech, a conclusion supported by the\nL103@P2: parties’ stipulations, including that Ms. Smith intends to produce a\nL104@P2: final story for each couple using her own words and original artwork.\nL105@P2: While Ms. Smith’s speech may combine with the couple’s in a final\nL106@P2: product, an individual “does not forfeit constitutional protection\nL107@P2: simply by combining multifarious voices” in a single communication.\nL108@P2: Hurley, 515 U. S., at 569.\nL109@P2: Ms. Smith seeks to engage in protected First Amendment speech;\nL110@P2: Colorado seeks to compel speech she does not wish to provide. As the\nL111@P2: Tenth Circuit observed, if Ms. Smith offers wedding websites celebrat\u0002ing marriages she endorses, the State intends to compel her to create\nL112@P2: custom websites celebrating other marriages she does not. 6 F. 4th\nL113@P2: 1160, 1178. Colorado seeks to compel this speech in order to “excis[e]\nL114@P2: certain ideas or viewpoints from the public dialogue.” Turner Broad\u0002casting System, Inc. v. FCC, 512 U. S. 633, 642. Indeed, the Tenth\nL115@P2: Circuit recognized that the coercive “[e]liminati[on]” of dissenting\nL116@P2: ideas about marriage constitutes Colorado’s “very purpose” in seeking\nL117@P2: to apply its law to Ms. Smith. 6 F. 4th, at 1178. But while the Tenth\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTravel American Utopias (U.S. National Park Service) (https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelamanacolonies/index.htm)\nciteturn66view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nps.gov/subjects/travelamanacolonies/index.htm\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 85\nL0: 【0†National Park Service】\nL1: \nL2: Search\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Keyword Search]\nL5: \nL6: [Button: This Site ] [Button: All NPS ]\nL7: \nL8: [Input] [Input]\nL9: \nL10: Menu Close\nL11: \nL12: ## Explore This Park\nL13: \nL14: ## Explore the National Park Service\nL15: \nL16: [Button: Cancel]\nL17: \nL18: # 【1†Travel American Utopias】\nL19: # Travel the Amana Colonies\nL20: The National Park Service invites you to explore stories, places, and the people of the Amana Colonies - an American Utopia. First founded in Germany in 1714, the Community of True Inspiration made east-central Iowa their home in 1855. The Inspirationists founded seven villages near the Iowa River and practiced a fully communal lifestyle until 1932. Today, visitors to the villages can see the area’s unique architecture and learn about the ways of life of the still-active communities.\nL21: Explore more 【2†Discover our Shared Heritage Travel Itineraries】 and learn about our diverse heritage by visiting 【3†Telling All Americans' Stories】.\nL22: \nL23: 【4†Communal Amana】\nL24: \nL25: 【5†Image: men standing on top of hay stacks behind horses and a tractor】\nL26: \nL27: The Amana Colonies were established as religious villages in rural Iowa. Learn about how Amana developed over the communal period\nL28: \nL29: 【6†Amana Colonies Today】\nL30: \nL31: 【7†Image: man checking a loom in the woolen mill】\nL32: 1932 marked the end of the communal period. Learn about what brought about \"the Great Change\" and how the Colonies adapted\nL33: \nL34: 【8†High Amana Store】\nL35: \nL36: 【9†Image: exterior of the high amana store after snow】\nL37: \nL38: General stores provided necessities to the people in the Amana Colonies. Learn about one of these stores in High Amana\nL39: \nL40: 【10†Hahn's Hearth Oven Bakery】\nL41: \nL42: 【11†Image: man taking bread out of the oven】\nL43: Bakeries provided bread and other baked goods to communal kitchens using traditionally German recipes. Learn about Hanh's in Amana\nL44: \nL45: 【12†Homestead Meat Shop and Smokehouse】\nL46: \nL47: 【13†Image: tower of the smokehouse】\nL48: \nL49: The Meat Shop and Smokehouse prepared meat for several kitchens in Homestead. Learn more about its role in the community\nL50: ## Visit Parks Related To Travel American Utopias\nL51: \nL52: Last updated: January 4, 2018\nL53: ### Tools\nL54: \nL55:   * 【14†Site Index】\nL56: \nL57: 【15†Image】\nL58: \nL59: [Input: Keyword Search]\nL60: \nL61:   * [Button: This Site ]\nL62:   * [Button: All NPS ]\nL63: [Input] [Input]\nL64: \nL65: Experience Your America.\nL66: \nL67: Download the NPS app to navigate the parks on the go.\nL68: \nL69: 【16†Image: Three smartphones showing different screens within the NPS app.】\nL70:   * 【17†Accessibility】\nL71:   * 【18†Privacy Policy】\nL72:   * 【19†FOIA】\nL73:   * 【20†Notices】\nL74:   * 【21†Contact The National Park Service】\nL75:   * 【22†NPS FAQ】\nL76:   * 【23†No Fear Act†www.doi.gov】\nL77:   * 【24†Disclaimer】\nL78:   * 【25†Vulnerability Disclosure Policy】\nL79:   * 【26†USA.gov†www.usa.gov】\nL80:   * 【27†Office of Civil Rights†www.doi.gov】\nL81: \nL82: Looking for U.S. government information and services?\nL83: \nL84: 【26†Visit USA.gov†www.usa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn66view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.socom.mil/TipOfTheSpear/USSOCOM%20Tip%20of%20the%20Spear%20August%202025.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.socom.mil/TipOfTheSpear/USSOCOM%20Tip%20of%20the%20Spear%20August%202025.pdf: (400) Content length is too large: 14104822"
